Subject: [PATCH 60/60] drivers/pci: pci-map-out, the other half of map-in -- a leak had been wearing the name "call-once-and-keep"

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

The 1275 PCI Bus Binding defines map-in and map-out as a PAIR. This firmware
bound only pci-map-in. Nobody noticed, because on x86/amd64 (no CONFIG_OFMEM)
map-in returns the physical address and claims nothing, so there was never
anything to give back -- and on ppc, where ob_pci_map() CLAIMS the physical and
virtual ranges through ofmem before mapping them, the symptom was written down
and given a name instead of a cause. #388's optrom track recorded: "a SECOND
call for the same region does not return a readable address, so it is
call-once-and-keep, not a getter."

It was a leak. The second call's ofmem_claim fails on the range the first call
still holds, and the caller gets -1 (ffffffff) for an address. Found by the
2026-09-03 audit of #388/#389, reading ob_pci_map() for what it does rather
than the track for what it said.

THE FIX IS THE INVERSE THAT ALREADY EXISTED. ofmem_release() is 6.3.2.4's
release: unmap, release virt, release phys -- exactly what ob_pci_map() did,
undone. pci-map-out ( virt size -- ) calls it under CONFIG_OFMEM and is a no-op
elsewhere, on the bus node; the bridge node chains it to its parent the way it
chains map-in (that hunk rides in patch 59, which reworks the bridge's table).
dsl/optrom.fth gains optrom-unmap, and optrom-run-mapped -- which until this
audit unmapped nothing and was called by nothing -- now maps, byte-loads, and
gives the mapping back on every exit path.

MEASURED on ppc (mac99, the `optrom` track's row F), in both directions:
    map-in                        800a0000   (MAP -- the address the property publishes)
    map-out, then map-in again    800a0000   (MAPX: a released region maps again, and
                                              the card's FCode byte-loads from it: MARK)
    map-in again, no release      ffffffff   (MAPY: the duplicate claim refused -- the
                                              leak, kept as the CONTROL that names it)

Also in this patch: forth_lb_table (patch 58) searched for the coreboot table
TWICE per call, once per pushed cell, tripling the debug chatter for nothing.
One search.

diff --git a/drivers/pci.c b/drivers/pci.c
--- a/drivers/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/pci.c
@@ -510,12 +510,36 @@
 static void ob_pci_config_w_write(int *idx) { forth_config_w_write(); }
 static void ob_pci_config_l_write(int *idx) { forth_config_l_write(); }
 
+/* ( virt size -- ) -- mklab, B.3 Spike 3 (patch 60): THE OTHER HALF OF MAP-IN.
+ * The 1275 PCI Bus Binding defines map-in and map-out as a pair; this firmware
+ * bound only the first. On ppc, ob_pci_map() CLAIMS the physical and virtual
+ * ranges through ofmem before mapping them, so with nothing to release them a
+ * second map-in of the same region fails its claim and hands back an address
+ * that reads nothing -- which the optrom track had recorded as "call-once-and-
+ * keep, not a getter". It was a leak with a nicer name. ofmem_release() (6.3.2.4)
+ * is the exact inverse of what ob_pci_map() did: unmap, release virt, release
+ * phys. Without CONFIG_OFMEM (x86/amd64) map-in returned the physical address
+ * and claimed nothing, so there is nothing to give back. */
+static void
+ob_pci_bus_map_out(int *idx)
+{
+	ucell size = POP();
+	ucell virt = POP();
+
+#if defined(CONFIG_OFMEM)
+	ofmem_release(virt, size);
+#else
+	(void)virt; (void)size;
+#endif
+}
+
 NODE_METHODS(ob_pci_bus_node) = {
 	{ "open",		ob_pci_open		},
 	{ "close",		ob_pci_close		},
 	{ "decode-unit",	ob_pci_decode_unit	},
 	{ "encode-unit",	ob_pci_encode_unit	},
 	{ "pci-map-in",		ob_pci_bus_map_in	},
+	{ "pci-map-out",	ob_pci_bus_map_out	},
 	{ "config-b@",		ob_pci_config_b_read	},
 	{ "config-w@",		ob_pci_config_w_read	},
 	{ "config-l@",		ob_pci_config_l_read	},
diff --git a/libopenbios/linuxbios_info.c b/libopenbios/linuxbios_info.c
--- a/libopenbios/linuxbios_info.c
+++ b/libopenbios/linuxbios_info.c
@@ -201,24 +201,17 @@
  * Addresses out are PHYSICAL, because x86 relocates and a bare pointer handed
  * to Forth would let a snapshot land on RAM that reads back convincingly.
  */
-static unsigned long lb_table_phys(void)
-{
-	struct lb_header *head = lb_find_table();
-
-	return head ? virt_to_phys(head) : 0;
-}
-
-static unsigned long lb_table_len(void)
+static void forth_lb_table(void)
 {
 	struct lb_header *head = lb_find_table();
 
-	return head ? (unsigned long)(sizeof(*head) + head->table_bytes) : 0;
-}
-
-static void forth_lb_table(void)
-{
-	PUSH(lb_table_phys());
-	PUSH(lb_table_len());
+	if (!head) {
+		PUSH(0);
+		PUSH(0);
+		return;
+	}
+	PUSH(virt_to_phys(head));
+	PUSH((ucell)(sizeof(*head) + head->table_bytes));
 }
 
 static void forth_lb_walk(void)
